  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-068',
    number: 281,
    month: 7,
    question: 'My scan mentioned a cord around my baby\'s neck. Should I be worried?',
    category: 'Health & Medical',
    applicability: 'Month 7 onward',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'Yes',
    held: true,
    heldNote: 'Yes, holds for Ishaan\'s sign-off (tone-sensitive; touches rare complications)',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'First, take a breath. We know how alarming those words sound when a sonographer says them, and it\'s completely natural that your mind went straight to the worst place. But a nuchal cord, the medical name for the umbilical cord looping around your baby\'s neck, is genuinely one of the most common findings in pregnancy, and for the overwhelming majority of babies, it causes no problems at all.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Your baby has spent months somersaulting, turning, and stretching in a space filled with fluid and a long, floating cord. With all that movement, the cord ends up looped somewhere fairly often; the neck is simply one of the places it can settle. Nothing you did caused this, and nothing you could have done would have prevented it. It\'s a matter of ordinary movement, not of anything going wrong.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Most loops sit loosely, without pressing on the cord or restricting anything, and your baby\'s own movements can just as easily unwrap it again before birth. Many cords seen at earlier scans are simply gone by the next one. For now, there is usually nothing you need to do differently, and no change to how your pregnancy is monitored.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor to explain what was seen and whether it changes anything for you; for most women, the answer is that it doesn\'t.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'This finding worries far more parents than it ever affects babies. It\'s worth knowing that your care team sees this constantly, and treats it as ordinary rather than concerning.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'A nuchal cord means the cord has looped around your baby\'s neck.',
        'It is very common and usually causes no problems at all.',
        'It happens from your baby\'s ordinary movement; nothing you did caused it.',
        'Most loops are loose, and many unwrap on their own before birth.',
        'It usually changes nothing about your monitoring or your plan.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-070',
      'PREG-HEALTH-071',
      'PREG-BABY-015',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BODY-063',
    number: 282,
    month: 7,
    question: 'Why are my hands tingling or numb (carpal tunnel syndrome)?',
    category: 'Body & Symptoms',
    applicability: 'Month 7 onward (third trimester)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Tingling, numbness, or aching in your hands, especially the thumb and first few fingers, is commonly carpal tunnel syndrome, affecting up to 6 in 10 pregnant women, most often in the third trimester. It\'s caused by fluid retention pressing on a nerve in your wrist and usually resolves within weeks of delivery.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Pregnancy naturally increases fluid retention throughout your body, and when that extra fluid builds up around your wrist, it compresses the median nerve running through a narrow passage there, called the carpal tunnel. This is why symptoms are often worse at night, when fluid redistributes and your wrist position during sleep can add further pressure.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Wearing a wrist splint, especially at night, and avoiding repetitive wrist movements or prolonged bent-wrist positions can genuinely help. Shaking out your hands or changing position when symptoms flare can also offer some relief in the moment.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'See your doctor if symptoms are significant, affect your grip or daily tasks, or don\'t ease with simple measures, since early treatment helps manage this well.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'This is a surprisingly common, lesser-talked-about pregnancy symptom; you\'re not imagining it, and it\'s genuinely manageable while it lasts.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Tingling or numbness in the hands affects up to 6 in 10 pregnant women.',
        'Most common in the third trimester, caused by fluid pressing on a wrist nerve.',
        'Often worse at night due to fluid shifts and wrist position.',
        'A wrist splint and avoiding repetitive wrist movements help.',
        'Usually resolves within weeks of delivery.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-043',
      'PREG-BODY-062',
      'PREG-BODY-050',
