  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-069',
    number: 283,
    month: 7,
    question: 'Is itchy skin normal in pregnancy, or could it be something else?',
    category: 'Health & Medical',
    applicability: 'Month 7 onward (from ~week 28)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'Yes',
    held: true,
    heldNote: 'Yes, holds for Ishaan\'s sign-off (genuine liver-condition safety content with real Indian-population relevance, per Escalation Guardrail)',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Mild itching, especially on your growing belly, is common and usually just from stretching skin. However, severe itching, particularly on your palms and soles, without a rash, can be a sign of a liver condition called intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy (ICP), which needs prompt medical attention, especially given it\'s notably more common in South Asian women.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Ordinary pregnancy itching comes from your skin stretching and hormonal changes affecting your skin\'s sensitivity, generally mild and manageable. ICP is different: it involves bile acids building up in your body instead of flowing normally, and while the exact cause isn\'t fully understood, it\'s known to run in families and typically appears from around 28 weeks, right where you are now, though it can start earlier.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'For ordinary itching, regular moisturiser usually helps, and it\'s nothing to worry about. But if you notice intense itching, especially on your palms or soles, without any visible rash, particularly if it\'s worse at night, tell your doctor within a day or two rather than assuming it\'s ordinary stretching. Your doctor can run simple blood tests to check your liver function and bile acid levels.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Contact your doctor within 24 hours for any new, notable itching, especially severe itching without a rash or itching concentrated on your palms and soles.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Most itching in pregnancy really is just your skin adjusting, but this is exactly the kind of symptom worth mentioning rather than assuming; a simple blood test gives real peace of mind either way.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Mild belly itching from skin stretching is common and usually harmless.',
        'Severe itching, especially on palms and soles without a rash, needs checking.',
        'This can signal ICP (intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy), a liver condition.',
        'ICP is notably more common in South Asian women.',
        'Contact your doctor within a day or two for any new, significant itching.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-BODY-038',
      'PREG-BODY-059',
      'PREG-TEST-002',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-PARTNER-005',
    number: 284,
    month: 7,
    question: 'How do I prepare my older child for a new sibling?',
    category: 'Partner & Relationships',
    applicability: 'Month 7 onward (commonly once pregnancy visibly shows)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Most experts suggest telling your child once your pregnancy starts to show, or naturally becomes part of daily conversation, giving them time to gradually adjust rather than a sudden announcement. Keep explanations simple, age-appropriate, and honest.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Children benefit from time to process big changes, and telling them early, while involving them in small parts of preparation, reduces anxiety and jealousy once the baby actually arrives. Young children don\'t yet understand abstract time, so referencing an event or season, "your baby brother will come after Diwali," works far better than "in a few months."',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Keep your explanations short and simple, and follow your child\'s lead on how much detail they want; some ask many questions, others very few. Reassure them clearly that your love for them isn\'t changing, and where possible, involve them in small, concrete preparations, choosing a toy for the baby, helping set up a room, so the change feels like something happening with them, not just to them.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'This is a family and parenting matter rather than a medical one; there\'s nothing to check with your doctor here.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Some regression, wanting a bottle again, seeking extra attention, once the baby arrives is a completely normal way for children to process this change, and it passes with patience and reassurance.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Tell your child once your pregnancy starts to show, or naturally comes up.',
        'Keep explanations simple, honest, and age-appropriate.',
        'Use an event or season as a time reference, not abstract timelines.',
        'Involve them in small, concrete preparations where possible.',
        'Regression after the baby arrives is normal and passes with patience.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-EMOTION-008',
      'PREG-PARTNER-002',
      'PREG-GS-006',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
