  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-070',
    number: 286,
    month: 7,
    question: 'Is there a difference between a single loop and multiple loops of cord around my baby\'s neck?',
    category: 'Health & Medical',
    applicability: 'Month 7 onward',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'Yes',
    held: true,
    heldNote: 'Yes, holds for Ishaan\'s sign-off (clinical nuance around loop count and tightness)',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Yes, though probably less than you\'d fear. A single loop is by far the most common, seen in up to 1 in 4 babies at birth, and rarely causes any issue. Multiple loops, two or more, are much less common, occurring in roughly 2 to 8 out of every 100 babies, and are watched a little more closely, though most of these babies are also completely fine.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'What actually matters clinically is less the number of loops and more whether the cord is loose or tight, and here\'s an honest thing worth knowing: ultrasound can usually tell how many loops there are, but it cannot reliably tell whether a cord is loose or tight. This is exactly why doctors rarely change anything based on the loop count alone, and why your baby\'s heart rate during labour, which shows how they\'re actually coping, matters far more than what a scan picture showed weeks earlier.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'If your report mentions one loop, this is genuinely ordinary. If it mentions two or more, your doctor may keep a slightly closer eye, but this still isn\'t a reason to expect a problem. Either way, your movements-monitoring and regular visits continue as normal.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor what your specific report showed and whether it changes your plan in any way.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'A number on a scan report can feel like a verdict, but here it\'s closer to a passing observation; what your baby does in labour tells your team far more than the count ever could.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'A single loop is most common, seen in up to 1 in 4 babies at birth.',
        'Multiple loops occur in roughly 2 to 8 out of 100 babies.',
        'Whether the cord is loose or tight matters more than the number of loops.',
        'Ultrasound cannot reliably tell whether a cord is tight.',
        'Most babies with either single or multiple loops are completely fine.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-068',
      'PREG-HEALTH-071',
      'PREG-HEALTH-067',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-071',
    number: 287,
    month: 7,
    question: 'If there\'s a cord around my baby\'s neck, will I need a caesarean?',
    category: 'Health & Medical',
    applicability: 'Month 7 onward',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'Yes',
    held: true,
    heldNote: 'Yes, holds for Ishaan\'s sign-off (delivery-mode content, per Escalation Guardrail)',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Almost certainly not, on its own. A nuchal cord, the cord looped around your baby\'s neck, is not by itself a reason for a caesarean delivery. One Indian study found that around 75 percent of babies with a nuchal cord were delivered vaginally with no complications for mother or baby.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Doctors don\'t plan deliveries around a cord seen on a scan, partly because the scan can\'t tell how loose or tight it is, and partly because most loops simply don\'t cause trouble. What guides decisions during labour is your baby\'s heart rate, which is monitored throughout, showing in real time how your baby is actually coping. A caesarean would only become necessary if your baby showed signs of distress, and even then the decision would rest on that, not on the cord finding itself.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'You can continue planning for the delivery you and your doctor were already planning. During labour, your delivery team monitors your baby\'s heart rate closely, and if a cord is found at birth, they are very well practised at handling it, usually simply slipping it over your baby\'s head as they\'re born.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor whether your specific findings change anything about your delivery plan; usually they don\'t.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'It\'s a relief to know this decision won\'t be made from a grainy scan image weeks in advance; it will be made by watching how your baby is genuinely doing, in the moment.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'A nuchal cord alone is not a reason for a caesarean.',
        'One Indian study found about 75 percent delivered vaginally without complications.',
        'Your baby\'s heart rate during labour guides decisions, not the scan finding.',
        'A caesarean would only follow signs of your baby being in distress.',
        'Delivery teams are well practised at safely managing a cord at birth.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-068',
      'PREG-HEALTH-070',
      'PREG-BABY-023',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-072',
    number: 288,
    month: 7,
    question: 'Should I opt for stem cell storage for my baby?',
    category: 'Health & Medical',
    applicability: 'Month 7 onward (decision before delivery)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'Yes (light, given the guilt attached to declining)',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'This is genuinely your decision, and families reasonably choose differently. What\'s worth knowing before you decide: major medical bodies, including the Indian Academy of Pediatrics, don\'t routinely recommend paying to privately store your baby\'s stem cells unless there\'s a specific family history of a condition treatable by a stem cell transplant. It\'s an optional extra rather than a part of routine pregnancy care.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Private storage is often marketed as "biological insurance" against future illness, and it\'s this specific framing that medical bodies don\'t endorse. Two facts rarely make it into the brochure. First, your baby\'s own stored stem cells generally cannot treat a genetic condition your baby was born with, because those same cells carry the same genetic change. Second, if a child later develops leukaemia, their own stored sample may carry the same early cells and wouldn\'t be used. The chance of a child ever using their own stored sample is low; estimates vary enormously across studies, but every source agrees it\'s small.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Private storage is most clearly worthwhile if a family member has a known condition treatable by a stem cell transplant, and it\'s worth asking your doctor whether anything in your history applies. Beyond that, it\'s a personal call: a significant cost against something statistically unlikely to be used, weighed against whatever peace of mind it gives you. There\'s no medical risk to your baby either way, and public donation is worth looking into as an alternative.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor whether anything in your family history would genuinely make private storage worthwhile for you specifically.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Whichever way you go, it\'s worth deciding on the facts rather than on the worry the marketing can create. Families who store and families who don\'t are both making reasonable choices.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'This is your decision; families reasonably choose differently.',
        'Medical bodies don\'t routinely recommend it without a relevant family history.',
        'Your baby\'s own cells can\'t treat a genetic condition they were born with.',
        'The chance of ever using a privately stored sample is low.',
        'There\'s no medical risk to your baby either way.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-076',
      'PREG-HEALTH-075',
      'PREG-HEALTH-074',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-CANI-DO-018',
    number: 289,
    month: 7,
    question: 'Can I still fly after week 28, and what documents will I need in India?',
    category: 'Work & Travel',
    applicability: 'Month 7 onward (from week 28)',
    canI: 'Do',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Yes, usually, but the rules change at exactly week 28. Up to 28 weeks you can fly without any documentation. From 28 to around 36 weeks, Indian airlines require a medical fitness certificate from your obstetrician confirming your pregnancy is uncomplicated and stating your expected delivery date. Most domestic airlines stop permitting travel after around 36 weeks.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'These rules exist mainly because of the small but real chance of going into labour, which airlines aren\'t equipped to manage mid-flight; they aren\'t a sign that flying itself is harmful to your baby. The Directorate General of Civil Aviation (DGCA) sets the baseline for Indian airlines, though individual airlines can and do apply stricter rules, and international routes often have earlier cutoffs, sometimes from 28 to 32 weeks depending on flight length.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Check your specific airline\'s policy before booking rather than assuming, since cutoffs and certificate requirements vary. The validity window for your certificate is short, and sources differ on exactly how short, so ask your airline directly and get the certificate close to your travel date. Carry it in original, on your doctor\'s letterhead, along with your pregnancy records.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor for a fitness certificate close to your travel date, and check whether they see any reason you shouldn\'t fly.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'The paperwork is a nuisance, but it\'s worth handling early; a certificate sorted a week ahead is far easier than a conversation at the check-in counter.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Up to 28 weeks, no documentation is needed to fly in India.',
        'From 28 to about 36 weeks, a medical fitness certificate is required.',
        'Most domestic airlines stop permitting travel after around 36 weeks.',
        'International routes often have earlier cutoffs, sometimes 28 to 32 weeks.',
        'Check your specific airline\'s policy and certificate validity window before booking.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-CANI-DO-010',
