      'PREG-SCAN-061',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-073',
    number: 290,
    month: 7,
    question: 'What is delayed cord clamping, and should I ask for it?',
    category: 'Labour & Birth Prep',
    applicability: 'Month 7 onward (decision before delivery)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Delayed cord clamping means waiting a short while after birth, commonly 1 to 3 minutes, before the umbilical cord is clamped and cut, letting more blood flow from your placenta into your baby. The World Health Organization recommends it for all births unless your baby needs immediate medical attention, and it\'s genuinely worth raising with your doctor before labour.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'In that first minute or so, your baby receives a meaningful extra volume of blood, which raises their haemoglobin and builds iron stores that measurably improve their iron status at 4 to 6 months of age. This matters particularly in India: the World Health Organization specifically favours longer delays in countries where iron deficiency is common, and anaemia in young children is widespread here. There is one honest trade-off worth knowing: delayed clamping slightly increases the chance of newborn jaundice needing light treatment, which is easily managed where that treatment is available.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Ask your doctor whether they practise delayed cord clamping and mention that you\'d like it if suitable. Guidance varies a little on timing, the World Health Organization suggests 1 to 3 minutes while some bodies suggest at least 30 to 60 seconds, but all agree some delay benefits your baby. If your baby needs urgent help at birth, immediate clamping is the right call, and your team will make that judgement.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Raise this before labour rather than during it, so your preference is already known and noted.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'There\'s something quietly generous about those extra sixty seconds; the placenta finishing its work, handing over one last gift of iron your baby will draw on for months.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Waiting 1 to 3 minutes before clamping lets more blood reach your baby.',
        'The World Health Organization recommends it for all births, barring emergencies.',
        'It improves your baby\'s iron stores at 4 to 6 months of age.',
        'This matters particularly in India, given widespread childhood anaemia.',
        'It slightly raises the chance of jaundice needing light treatment.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-074',
      'PREG-HEALTH-072',
      'PREG-BABY-015',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-074',
    number: 291,
    month: 7,
    question: 'Can I do both delayed cord clamping and cord blood banking?',
    category: 'Labour & Birth Prep',
    applicability: 'Month 7 onward (decision before delivery)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    held: true,
    heldNote: 'Yes, holds for Ishaan\'s sign-off (the FAQ explicitly notes commercial bias in available sources; worth your read)',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Partly, and this is a genuine trade-off worth understanding rather than a myth to be waved away. The two compete for the same blood: the longer you delay clamping, the more blood goes to your baby and the less remains to collect for banking. A short delay of around 60 seconds can often allow both, but longer delays make collecting a usable sample unlikely.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Within roughly the first minute after birth, your baby receives the large majority of the blood that would transfer from the cord, while the volume collectable for banking is cut by around half. Delay much beyond that and there is often too little left to meet a bank\'s minimum requirements. It\'s worth knowing that much of the "there\'s no conflict" messaging comes from cord blood banks themselves, who have a commercial interest in the answer; more neutral sources describe a real, if manageable, trade-off.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'If you\'re banking privately and also want delayed clamping, discuss a specific timing with your doctor and your chosen bank in advance, and understand that the sample may not meet their standard. If you\'re weighing the two, it\'s worth noting that the benefit of delayed clamping to your baby is well established, while the chance of ever using privately banked cord blood is low.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Discuss your preference on both well before labour, since this cannot be sorted out in the delivery room.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'This is a decision where knowing who\'s telling you what matters as much as what they\'re telling you; both options are safe, but only one has clear, immediate benefit for your baby.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'The two compete for the same blood; this is a real trade-off.',
        'A delay of around 60 seconds may allow both, with a reduced sample.',
        'Longer delays make collecting a usable banking sample unlikely.',
        'Much "no conflict" messaging comes from banks with a commercial interest.',
        'Delayed clamping\'s benefit is well established; private banking\'s use is rare.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-073',
      'PREG-HEALTH-072',
      'PREG-HEALTH-054',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-075',
    number: 292,
    month: 7,
    question: 'What is stem cell storage, and how is it actually done?',
    category: 'Health & Medical',
    applicability: 'Month 7 onward (arrange before delivery)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Stem cell storage means collecting the blood left inside your baby\'s umbilical cord and placenta after birth, which is unusually rich in stem cells, then processing and freezing it for possible future medical use. This blood was historically discarded. The collection itself is quick, painless, and safe for both you and your baby.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, 'How It Works', [], [
        'You register with a bank during pregnancy and complete paperwork covering your medical and family history.',
        'The bank sends you a sterile collection kit, which you must bring with you to the hospital when you deliver.',
        'After your baby is born and the cord is clamped and cut, your doctor or a collection nurse draws the blood remaining in the cord and placenta. Nothing is taken from your baby.',
        'A blood sample is usually taken from you as well, for screening.',
        'The kit is couriered to the bank\'s laboratory, which by regulation must happen within 72 hours, kept below 25 degrees Celsius.',
        'The laboratory tests, processes, and freezes the sample for long-term storage.',
      ]),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, 'What to Check Before Choosing a Bank', [], [
        'A licence from the Drugs Controller General of India (DCGI). This is mandatory; unlicensed banks can be shut down, and samples can be lost.',
        'Accreditation from a recognised body such as AABB (the American Association of Blood Banks).',
        'Their actual track record of releasing samples for real transplants, not just how many they store.',
        'Total cost, including collection, processing, and yearly storage fees.',
        'What happens to your sample if the bank closes down.',
      ]),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Tell your doctor and hospital in advance if you\'re planning this, since your delivery team does the collection.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'The process itself is genuinely simple; the harder part is choosing well, and that\'s about licences and track record rather than the brochure.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Blood is collected from the cord and placenta after birth, not from your baby.',
        'Register during pregnancy; bring the collection kit to the hospital.',
        'Your delivery team does the collection; it\'s painless and quick.',
        'The sample must reach the laboratory within 72 hours.',
        'Check for a DCGI licence and recognised accreditation before choosing.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-072',
      'PREG-HEALTH-076',
      'PREG-HEALTH-074',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BODY-064',
    number: 293,
    month: 7,
    question: 'Why do my ribs hurt in the third trimester, and what helps?',
    category: 'Body & Symptoms',
    applicability: 'Month 7 onward (from week 28)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Rib pain is very common from around week 28, as your womb grows tall enough to press upward against your rib cage and diaphragm. Once your baby settles head-down, their feet often end up tucked right under your ribs, which is why those kicks can feel so pointed. It\'s almost always muscle and pressure related, not a sign of damage.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Three things stack up together: your womb pushing your organs upward, your rib cage itself flaring outward to make room, and the hormone relaxin loosening the ligaments between your ribs, which leaves them more mobile and more tender. Add a baby who now has less space and stronger legs, and the ache makes complete sense.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, 'How to Manage It', [], [
        'Sit tall with your shoulders back, which opens up the chest and reduces rib compression',
        'Stretch your arms overhead for a moment; this genuinely creates more space in your torso',
        'If your baby is lodged against your ribs, shifting position or rolling over often moves them',
        'Tuck a rolled towel or small pillow behind your lower back when sitting, to stop you slouching forward',
        'Switch to a well-fitting, non-underwire bra, since extra pressure from outside doesn\'t help',
      ]),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Tell your doctor about pain that is severe, persistent, sits under your ribs on the upper right side, or comes with breathlessness, fever, nausea, or yellowing of your skin or eyes.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'There\'s some relief coming: when your baby drops lower into your pelvis nearer delivery, the pressure on your ribs usually eases noticeably.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Very common from week 28 as your womb reaches rib height.',
        'Your baby\'s feet often tuck under your ribs once they\'re head-down.',
        'Relaxin makes your ribs more mobile and more tender.',
        'Sitting tall, stretching up, and shifting position all help.',
        'Pain under the upper right ribs specifically needs checking.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-BODY-056',
      'PREG-HEALTH-039',
      'PREG-BODY-055',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BODY-065',
    number: 294,
    month: 7,
    question: 'Why are my dreams so vivid and strange in pregnancy?',
    category: 'Sleep',
    applicability: 'Month 7 onward (third trimester)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Vivid, intense, sometimes bizarre dreams are one of the most commonly reported experiences in pregnancy, and they often peak in the third trimester. They\'re driven by a mix of hormones, broken sleep, and your mind quietly working through a very big life change. They\'re normal, and they don\'t mean anything is wrong.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Progesterone, high throughout pregnancy, is associated with more time in the dreaming stage of sleep and with more emotionally charged dream content. Rising estrogen affects the brain chemistry that regulates that same sleep stage. On top of this, you\'re simply waking more often now, for the bathroom, for comfort, because of your baby\'s movements, and waking during or just after a dream is exactly what makes it stick in your memory. Nothing about your dream frequency has necessarily changed; your recall of them has.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'There\'s nothing you need to do about this, and no meaning you need to decode. Unsettling dreams about your baby\'s safety or about labour are extremely common in early third trimester and tend to reflect ordinary anticipation rather than intuition or warning. Roughly a third of pregnant women report frequent nightmares, most often in these final months.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Mention it if distressing dreams are regularly disrupting your sleep or leaving you anxious during the day.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Your mind is rehearsing something enormous, in the only way minds know how. Strange dreams are less a symptom to manage than a sign of how much you\'re already carrying.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Vivid dreams are extremely common and often peak in the third trimester.',
        'Progesterone and estrogen both affect the dreaming stage of sleep.',
        'Waking more often means you simply remember dreams more.',
        'Dreams about your baby\'s safety or labour are common and not warnings.',
        'Mention it if distressing dreams are regularly disrupting your sleep.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-BODY-062',
      'PREG-EMOTION-014',
      'PREG-BODY-054',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-076',
    number: 295,
    month: 7,
    question: 'What\'s the difference between public and private stem cell storage?',
    category: 'Health & Medical',
    applicability: 'Month 7 onward (decision before delivery)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Private storage means paying a company to keep your baby\'s stem cells reserved for your own family\'s use. Public donation means giving them free to a shared pool, where they may help anyone who matches, including strangers. The Indian Academy of Pediatrics and the Indian Medical Association both recommend public donation over private storage for families without a specific medical need.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Public banks charge nothing, are held to strict standards, and their samples are actually used; this is where cord blood transplants genuinely come from. Private banks are commercial services storing a sample for a family that will most likely never need it, and their samples are used far less often. There is one genuinely honest Indian caveat here: public cord blood banking infrastructure in India is limited compared to many other countries, so the recommended option may not be readily available to you.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Ask your hospital directly what public donation options exist where you\'re delivering, since availability varies considerably across India and you may find there are none nearby. If you already have a child with a condition treatable by a stem cell transplant, ask your doctor about directed donation, which is a distinct and genuinely encouraged route where a sibling\'s cells are stored specifically for that child.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor or hospital what public donation options are available locally, and whether directed donation applies to your family.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'The irony worth knowing is that the option medical bodies actually recommend is the one nobody advertises, precisely because there\'s no money in it.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Private storage: you pay; the sample is reserved for your family.',
        'Public donation: free; the sample joins a shared pool for anyone who matches.',
        'Indian and international medical bodies recommend public donation.',
        'Public cord blood banking infrastructure in India is genuinely limited.',
        'Directed donation is a distinct route if a sibling has a treatable condition.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-072',
      'PREG-HEALTH-075',
      'PREG-HEALTH-074',
    ],
  ),
];
